$CATEGORY: awareness-all-staff

::Note before the following questions::
The following questions come from daily work. They are not about repeating a
rule but about recognising, in a situation, what to do and whom to speak to.

// 1

::A message that pushes::
A message demands something immediately, arrives by an unusual route and rules
out questions. What is the first step?{
=Do not click the link and check the usual way. #Right.
~Open the link to see what it is about. #Opening it is the step the message is
after.
~Reply to the message and ask. #The reply goes to the sender of the message and
not to the person it claims to be about.
~Wait and see whether somebody else speaks up. #Waiting is the one response that
certainly improves nothing.
####The three marks together are the pattern. Each on its own occurs in genuine
messages too; all three together rarely do.}

// 2

::Reporting although it was genuine::
A reported message turns out to be genuine. That was a mistake.{FALSE
####Wrong. A genuine message reported costs a few minutes. A false one not
reported costs the time in which nobody can do anything.}

// 3

::The second factor on the phone::
Who may the second factor be told to?{
=Nobody.
=nobody
=No one
####Not your own IT department either. Anyone asking for it is presenting
themselves as somebody who would not need to.}

// 4

::A stand-in during leave::
A colleague goes on leave and her cases have to carry on. What is right?{
=The access administration sets up an account of its own for the stand-in.
#Right.
~She passes her password to the stand-in. #Then it is no longer possible to tell
who did what.
~She passes the password on only by word of mouth and changes it afterwards.
#That too removes the attribution for as long as it holds.
~The stand-in works under the department account. #A shared account is the same
case, larger.
####An account belongs to exactly one person, because otherwise nobody can say
who acted.}

// 5

::Loss of a device::
A work phone is left on a train on Saturday. When is it reported?{
=Immediately, at the weekend too. #Right.
~On Monday morning, because nobody is there before then. #The time until Monday
is the time without a response.
~Only once it is clear it is not in lost property. #The report does not rule out
the search.
~Only if work data was on it. #Neither the finder nor the loser decides that.
####Between loss and report nobody can do anything, and it is exactly that time
that can be shortened.}

// 6

::Two rules before sending::
Which two questions belong before every send of data?{
~%50%Does the recipient need this at all. #Right.
~%50%Do they need it in full, or is an extract enough. #Right.
~%-100%Is the file small enough to attach. #A question of technology and not of
security.
~%-100%Is the format readable for the recipient. #A question of technology as
well.
####Both questions cost seconds and prevent the commonest incident there is,
namely too much to too many.}

// 7

::After the click::
It has been clicked and the password entered. What is the first step?{
=Report it, immediately. #Right.
~Switch the device off. #That loses traces the response needs.
~Clear the history. #That hinders the response and changes nothing about the
incident.
~Wait and see whether anything happens. #Until then the response has not
started.
####The report comes before everything else. The password is changed afterwards,
and on another device.}

// 8

::Private storage::
Work data may sit on a private storage device as long as nobody passes it on.
{FALSE
####Wrong. What belongs to the organisation or to its customers stays in the
routes the organisation provides for it.}

// 9

::On a change of role::
What belongs to a move into another role?{
=Saying yourself which accounts are no longer needed. #Right.
~Keeping the accounts in case questions come up. #That is exactly how the old
account that stands open later arises.
~Nothing, human resources handles it by itself. #They hear about the move, but
not which accounts arose in daily work.
~Passing the accounts on to your successor. #Accounts are set up anew and not
handed over.
####A change of role leaves more open accounts behind than a departure, because
at a departure somebody looks.}

// 10

::Your own situation::
Describe in two sentences a situation from your own daily work in which your own
behaviour decides about the security of information, and whom you speak to in
it.{
####Model answer. What is expected is a concrete situation from your own work
rather than an example from the material, and with it the place in the house that
is spoken to in that situation. Anyone finding no situation has not yet carried
the question over to their own work.}
